[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last question. [1mThe fork:[0m what happens when the page loads on a hostname that isn't in the map — a preview deploy, a
  new CDN alias, an IP address, a typo'd staging host.

  - [1mThrow at load with a clear message[0m — the app refuses to run rather than guess. You find out the instant you hit an
    unlisted host, and the fix is one map entry. Cost: an unlisted [3mproduction[0m alias takes the page down completely for
    real users, so the map becomes something you must keep current with your deploy hosts.
  - [1mFall back to production, log a warning[0m — the page always works. Cost: the failure is silent. A mistyped staging
    hostname means your development session posts real credentials to the production API and nothing tells you except a
    console line nobody's reading.
  - [1mFall back to local, log a warning[0m — never touches prod by accident. Cost: any unlisted host is quietly broken for
    actual users, with a confusing localhost-connection error instead of a clear one.

  [1mMy recommendation: throw at load.[0m This is a login form, so the wrong-endpoint failure mode is credentials going to the
  wrong API — and the production fallback makes exactly that mistake silently. A thrown error is loud, immediate, and
  fixed by adding one line to a three-entry map. The real cost is that you have to add new production hostnames to the
  map before deploying to them; if your prod hosts change often or you use preview deploys with generated URLs, the
  production fallback is the more practical answer and I'd switch.
